[1, 2, 4, 1, 3, 4]

Sort:

[1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4]

Create linked list:

1 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 4
Complexity
Time: O((n + m) log(n + m))
Space: O(n + m)

Approach 2 — Using ArrayList Without Sorting

We already know both lists are sorted.

So we can perform the same merge operation as merge sort:

Compare current elements
        ↓
Take smaller
        ↓
Move that list forward
        ↓
Repeat

Instead of directly connecting nodes, store the result in an ArrayList.

Code
class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode list1, ListNode list2) {

        ArrayList<Integer> arr = new ArrayList<>();

        while (list1 != null && list2 != null) {

            if (list1.val <= list2.val) {
                arr.add(list1.val);
                list1 = list1.next;
            } else {
                arr.add(list2.val);
                list2 = list2.next;
            }
        }

        while (list1 != null) {
            arr.add(list1.val);
            list1 = list1.next;
        }

        while (list2 != null) {
            arr.add(list2.val);
            list2 = list2.next;
        }

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode curr = dummy;

        for (int value : arr) {
            curr.next = new ListNode(value);
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}

