Why Do We Use a Dummy Node?

Without a dummy node, we would need to separately determine the first node of the merged list.

For example:

1 → 2 → 4
1 → 3 → 4

Which 1 should become the head?

A dummy node eliminates this special case:

dummy → ...

Every selected node simply gets attached using:

list.next = selectedNode;

At the end:

return head.next;
Why Can We Attach the Remaining List Directly?

Suppose:

list1 = 7 → 8 → 10
list2 = null

Because list1 was already sorted, we don't need to compare anything anymore.

The remaining portion can simply be attached:

result → 7 → 8 → 10

That's why these loops are useful:

while (list1 != null) {
    list.next = list1;
    list1 = list1.next;
    list = list.next;
}

while (list2 != null) {
    list.next = list2;
    list2 = list2.next;
    list = list.next;
}

Actually, after the first main loop, you can make the code even shorter:

list.next = (list1 != null) ? list1 : list2;
Slightly Cleaner Optimal Version

Same algorithm, fewer lines:

class Solution {
    public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode list1, ListNode list2) {

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode curr = dummy;

        while (list1 != null && list2 != null) {

            if (list1.val <= list2.val) {
                curr.next = list1;
                list1 = list1.next;
            } else {
                curr.next = list2;
                list2 = list2.next;
            }

            curr = curr.next;
        }

        curr.next = (list1 != null) ? list1 : list2;

        return dummy.next;
    }
}

This is the version I'd use in an interview.

